<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Jacques Dessalines was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. After the defeat of the French expedition, he declared the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804 and became its first ruler. The loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> was a major blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Dessalines" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Rigaud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Jacques_Dessalines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dessalines was born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, as Jean-Jacques Duclos, on the Cormier plantation near Grande-Rivière-du-Nord in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. The names of his parents and their region of origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> are not known. He rose to commandeur, or foreman, and worked there until he was about 30. He was then bought by a free man of colour named Dessalines, whose surname he took and kept after he gained his freedom. When the uprising of 1791 began on the Plaine-du-Nord, he joined it,.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Dessalines became a lieutenant in the army of Jean François Papillon and followed him into Spanish service, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1794 Louverture switched to the French, and Dessalines followed, becoming his chief lieutenant and a brigadier general by 1799. He took several towns, and in 1801 quickly ended an insurrection led by Louverture's nephew Moyse, gaining a reputation for taking no prisoners and burning villages. When General Charles Leclerc's expedition arrived in 1802, he defended the fort of Crête-à-Pierrot with 1,300 men against 18,000 attackers on 11 March, and after a 20-day siege broke out with his army largely intact.</p><p>He briefly sided with Leclerc afterwards, and several historians hold him partly responsible for Louverture's arrest in June 1802. When the French plan to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> became clear he changed sides again in October 1802. By November he led the alliance with the blessing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a>, and he became commander of the Indigenous Army on 18 May 1803. Leclerc died of yellow fever, and the harsh methods of his successor Donatien de Rochambeau united the rebels. The decisive battle was Vertières on 18 November 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 1 January 1804 at Gonaïves Dessalines declared independence and named the country <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Ayiti</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> name. He was governor-general for life until the generals proclaimed him Emperor Jacques I on 22 September 1804; he was crowned at Le Cap on 6 October. Between February and April 1804 he ordered the massacre of the remaining French population, killing between 3,000 and 5,000 people. Some historians cite the threat of a French reinvasion and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. He spared Polish Legionnaires who had defected, and made them citizens. The 1805 constitution confirmed the abolition of slavery and forbade white colonists to own property.</p><p>He enforced plantation labour, called agrarian militarism by the historian Michel-Rolph Trouillot, and many newly freed people felt it resembled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. He was assassinated at Larnage, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, on 17 October 1806 by members of his own administration, among them Pétion and Étienne-Élie Gérin, while on his way to put down a revolt in the south. A mob then dismembered his body, which a vivandière, Dédée Bazile, buried. Accounts of the killing differ.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Jacques Dessalines” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Haitian Revolution of 1791 to 1804 destroyed Saint-Domingue and produced the first Black republic in history under the leadership of Toussaint Louverture and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Haiti, the Taíno name for Hispaniola, was reclaimed in 1804 by <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b> for the state the enslaved people of French Saint-Domingue had won.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Haiti arose from the crisis that followed the assassination of Emperor Jacques I, the former <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b>, near Port-au-Prince on 17 October 1806.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain and Britain invaded; 7,000 French Republican troops landed in September 1792; Toussaint Louverture and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b>, leading the formerly leaderless revolt, first allied with Spain, then destroyed the Spanish forces and joined the Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>France conceded defeat in 1803, and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b> proclaimed the republic of Haiti.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1802 Napoleon sent an army to retake the colony, and French forces restored slavery in Guadeloupe, but Saint-Domingue’s Black and mixed-race generals defeated the expedition, and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b> proclaimed the independence of Haiti on 1 January 1804.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Grande-Rivière-du-Nord, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.58,-72.18&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Grande-Rivière-du-Nord on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jean-Jacques Dessalines"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4026 5834h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4026 5834h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.58,-72.18&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
